# Per-profile API keys for services the agent drives (web-read broker, etc.).
#
# Copy this file to your profile's state dir as `secrets.env`:
#   cp sandbox_templates/common/secrets.env.template \
#      ~/.ai-sandbox/profiles/<profile>/secrets.env
# then fill in the keys you need and delete the lines you don't.
#
# `scripts/profile.sh <profile> up` also drops a `secrets.env.example` copy of
# this template into the profile dir for convenience.
#
# SECURITY / MECHANICS
#   * This file is injected as an OPTIONAL env_file (required: false) into the
#     agent container — same mechanism as db.env. profile.sh chmod 600s it.
#   * env_file is read only at container CREATE. After editing, recreate:
#       scripts/profile.sh <p> recreate
#     A plain `up` will NOT re-read this file for an already-running agent.
#   * Keys are read from the environment by the `webfetch` broker and never
#     appear on argv, so they stay out of the Bash-tool transcript and the
#     Squid URL log. Keep this file out of git — it lives under ~/.ai-sandbox,
#     outside the repo tree.
#   * Each service's HOST must be in proxy/allowed_domains.txt for the key to
#     be usable. Every broker backend's host is allowlisted in the [web-read]
#     block (Tavily, Firecrawl, TinyFish, Jina).
#   * VARIABLE NAMES ARE NOT A MATTER OF TASTE. Each one is whatever its
#     consumer actually reads; a plausible-looking synonym fails silently as a
#     missing key. Names below are cited to their call site — check there, not
#     the vendor's marketing copy, before renaming anything.

# --- Tavily (search + extract; api.tavily.com — already allowlisted) ---------
# Get a key at https://app.tavily.com/  (free tier available). Format: tvly-...
TAVILY_API_KEY=__SET_ME__

# --- TinyFish (search + fetch; api.search/api.fetch.tinyfish.ai — allowlisted
# [web-read]) — OPTIONAL --------------------------------------------------------
# Read by webfetch --via tinyfish (sandbox_templates/bin/webfetch). Search and
# Fetch are the vendor's free tier. The SAME key also unlocks their paid Agent
# and Browser APIs (a cloud browser the caller drives) — the broker never calls
# those and their hosts are not allowlisted; do not "connect" the TinyFish MCP
# inside a profile to get them. Get a key at https://agent.tinyfish.ai/api-keys
# Format: sk-tinyfish-...
# TINYFISH_API_KEY=sk-tinyfish-...

# --- Jina Reader (r.jina.ai / s.jina.ai — allowlisted [web-read]) — OPTIONAL -
# Read by webfetch --via jina (sandbox_templates/bin/webfetch). Keyless works
# (rate-limited); a key raises limits. Format: jina_...
# JINA_API_KEY=jina_...

# --- Firecrawl (api.firecrawl.dev — allowlisted [web-read]) — OPTIONAL -------
# Read by webfetch --via firecrawl (sandbox_templates/bin/webfetch). Note .dev,
# not .com. Format: fc-...
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=fc-...

# --- Hugging Face (huggingface.co + its CDN — GATED, [comfyui-models]) — OPTIONAL
# Only needed for GATED or private repos. Public weights download with no key.
#
# HF_TOKEN is the canonical name: huggingface_hub reads it and it OVERRIDES a
# token stored by `hf auth login` (_login.py:187,201, read in the profile venv
# at huggingface_hub 1.29.0). HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN is recognised as a legacy
# alias (_login.py:158) — prefer HF_TOKEN. Create a READ token at
# https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens  Format: hf_...
#
# A TOKEN ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH for a gated repo: the licence must also be
# accepted on the model page by the account that owns the token. Measured
# 2026-08-29 — black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-schnell answers HTTP 401 with
# `x-error-code: GatedRepo` until both are done. That 401 is Hugging Face's,
# NOT Squid's: a proxy refusal is TCP_DENIED/403 in access.log, and the log
# showed TCP_TUNNEL/200 for the same request. Squid never emits a 401.
#
# ComfyUI-Manager CANNOT USE THIS. Its downloader is unauthenticated urllib —
# no HF_TOKEN, Authorization or Bearer handling anywhere in its tree — so the
# Manager UI fails on gated models whatever is set here. Fetch those with the
# hub CLI in the workspace venv instead:
#   HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1 .venv/bin/hf download <repo> <file> --local-dir <dir>
# HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1 is not optional in practice: it keeps the transfer on
# the classic CDN host that IS allowlisted, where Xet redirects to a xethub CAS
# host that is not, and the download dies part-way with a TCP_DENIED.
#
# ALTERNATIVE THAT NEEDS NO RECREATE: `hf auth login` inside the container
# stores the token at $HF_HOME/token (default ~/.cache/huggingface/token,
# constants.py:247) — the profile's cache bind mount, so it survives
# `docker rm` without this file and without the container recreate that any
# env_file edit requires.
# HF_TOKEN=hf_...

# --- fal.ai hosted inference + MCP (GATED, [fal]) — OPTIONAL -----------------
# FAL_KEY is the canonical name, and the split form is the LEGACY one, not a
# synonym. Measured in the client sources, not the marketing copy:
#   * Python fal_client auth.py `_resolve_env_or_colab_auth` reads FAL_KEY
#     first, then FAL_KEY_ID + FAL_KEY_SECRET (both required together), and
#     builds the header as `Authorization: Key <value>` — scheme "Key", NOT
#     "Bearer". Its own missing-credentials string names exactly those three.
#   * JS @fal-ai/client config.ts reads process.env.FAL_KEY, else joins
#     `${FAL_KEY_ID}:${FAL_KEY_SECRET}` with a colon.
# So FAL_KEY takes the combined `<key-id>:<key-secret>` value. Setting only one
# half of the split pair reads as unset — the pair is checked with AND.
# Create one in the fal dashboard under Keys.
#
# THE HOSTS LIVE IN A GATED BLOCK. [fal] in proxy/allowed_domains.txt ships
# commented out; while it is, the key is inert until the block is opened for
# the command:
#   scripts/with-egress.sh <p> --with fal -- '<cmd>'
# The tag is `fal`, not `fal.ai` — a dot makes the block unopenable.
#
# THIS KEY BUYS COMPUTE, NOT A READ. fal runs models on its GPUs: whatever is
# submitted leaves the boundary (prompts, and any image or video passed as
# INPUT), the media hosts return bytes nothing in this repo inspects, and it is
# metered — an unattended loop costs money as well as egress. Same rule as the
# webfetch broker: the key travels in the Authorization header and never in a
# URL, because Squid logs URLs and a key in a query string is a leak the agent
# cannot see.
#
# The fal MCP endpoint (mcp.fal.ai) authenticates with the same key. Registering
# it is a separate decision — the key alone does not connect it.
# FAL_KEY=<key-id>:<key-secret>

# --- ClickUp personal API token — OPTIONAL -----------------------------------
# CLICKUP_TOKEN is the canonical name: it is what myclickup reads
# (src/myclickup/config.py, TOKEN_VAR). CLICKUP_API_TOKEN is accepted as a
# DEPRECATED alias; CLICKUP_API_KEY is wrong and reads as unset — that mistake
# was already made once and renamed out. ClickUp's own docs call this a
# personal API token, never a key. Format: pk_...
#
# Per-account variants CLICKUP_TOKEN_<ACCOUNT> (e.g. CLICKUP_TOKEN_ACME)
# take precedence when CLICKUP_ACCOUNT selects that account.
#
# Not needed for the claude.ai-hosted ClickUp MCP connector, which is OAuth and
# holds its own token. This is for the CLI/API client path only.
# CLICKUP_TOKEN=pk_...

# --- paperbridge (literature CLI; hosts live in [citation-tools]) — OPTIONAL --
# Read by the vendored `paperbridge` CLI. Names cited to the call site, not the
# vendor's docs: every one below is a `validation_alias` in
# src/paperbridge/_config.py (PaperBridgeSettings), read at 0.2.1.
#
# THE .env ROUTE IS CLOSED ON PURPOSE, so this file is the only way in.
# paperbridge disables pydantic-settings' `.env` loading whenever SANDBOX_PROFILE
# is set (its ADR-0003): the sandbox denies `.env` at the tool layer while the
# file is still sitting on a bind mount, and paperbridge's reads are
# allow-listed — so an allow-listed tool reading `.env` would be a *sanctioned*
# path for a denied read. Nothing to configure; just know that dropping a `.env`
# in the workspace will silently do nothing inside a container.
#
# A key is never echoed. `paperbridge config` reports each setting as
# `set`/`unset` and nothing else — not even a last-four suffix, which is still
# key material. If it ever prints one, that is a defect to report upstream.
#
# Zotero — create a key at https://www.zotero.org/settings/keys ; the userID is
# at the top of that page, a group ID is in the group's URL. Provide the key
# plus ONE of the two ids; group takes precedence if both are set. Needed by
# every `zotero-*` command, all eight writes included.
# ZOTERO_API_KEY=
# ZOTERO_USER_ID=
# ZOTERO_GROUP_ID=
#
# MY_EMAIL — the contact address CrossRef's polite pool wants and Unpaywall
# REQUIRES. `UNPAYWALL_EMAIL` is accepted as an alias by the same field; prefer
# MY_EMAIL, which is what the tool's own .env.example names.
# MY_EMAIL=you@example.com
#
# NCBI_API_KEY — optional; raises the PubMed/E-utilities rate limit from 3 to
# 10 req/s. Register at https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/account/
# NCBI_API_KEY=
#
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT / MAX_RETRIES are also read here (defaults 30 and 3). They are
# not secrets and are listed only so a reader does not go hunting for where the
# HTTP behaviour is set.

# --- OpenRouter key + the Claude Code backend switch — OPTIONAL ------------
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY is read by `scripts/profile.sh <p> backend openrouter`,
# which COPIES it into the profile's managed backend.env as the bearer token
# Claude Code sends. Nothing in the container reads this name directly; the
# name is fixed so the command can find it. Format: sk-or-v1-...
# openrouter.ai is already live in proxy/allowed_domains.txt — nothing to open.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-v1-...
#
# DO NOT write ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL / ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN / ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
# here by hand. The switch is a command, and it owns a separate file:
#   scripts/profile.sh <p> backend ollama --model qwen3-coder [--recreate]
#   scripts/profile.sh <p> backend openrouter --model anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5
#   scripts/profile.sh <p> backend anthropic
#   scripts/profile.sh <p> backend status
# (`just backend <p> ...` is the alias.) It writes backend.env next to this
# file, injected AFTER it, so a value here would be overridden anyway. The
# command sets ANTHROPIC_API_KEY to empty on purpose: a real value there is
# sent as x-api-key and treated as a direct-Anthropic credential, i.e. your
# Anthropic key would travel to whatever host the base URL names.
#
# Same create-time rule as every key in this file — nothing changes until
#   scripts/profile.sh <p> recreate      (or pass --recreate to the command)
# `verify` prints which backend the profile is on.
#
# Why a command and not this file: the settings template's `env` block is
# sandbox-owned and overwritten on every `up` (ADR-0007), and this file is
# operator-owned and never rewritten by a script — so the switch needed a
# third, managed home. Three near-copies of this file were rejected: every
# key added later would have to land in all of them.
#
# DOCUMENTED CAVEATS (work/0023 §8, measured where it says so):
#   * Anthropic documents the gateway mechanism but names NEITHER vendor; Ollama
#     and OpenRouter each document the recipe themselves.
#   * A non-first-party base URL turns MCP tool search off by default and makes
#     Remote Control and server-managed settings unavailable; model IDs pass
#     through unvalidated, so a typo is a request-time error.
#   * Community measurements put local-model edit accuracy around 70-80% against
#     ~98% for Sonnet; OpenRouter's own caveat is that Claude Code is optimised
#     for Anthropic models.
#   * Ollama recommends >=32K context; the sibling sets OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH=32768.
#     Claude Code assumes 200k for a model it does not recognise; pass
#     --context <tokens> to the command to set CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS.
#   * ollama/ollama#13949 (count_tokens 404 wedging the server): measured
#     2026-09-03 on the pinned 0.33.3 image — still 404, server does NOT wedge.
#   * What does NOT change: every control here is on the harness, not the model
#     — deny lists, the hook engine, seccomp, the egress allowlist.
